<tal:root define="member python:options['member']"
>From: <span tal:replace="structure view/encoded_mail_sender" />
To: <span tal:replace="python:member.getProperty('email')" />
Subject: SITE LAYER WINS
Content-Type: text/plain

data-sitelayer-override=mail_password_template

This text comes from a site package jbot directory, registered on a browser
layer that extends IEmailkitLayer. If you are reading it in a test assertion,
SPEC 8.2 level 1 holds.

The member lookup above is deliberate: it uses the hosting view namespace
(options/... and a python: expression), so a render proves the override was
really executed by the stock view and not merely resolved on disk.

Three authoring notes, all learned the hard way while writing this file:
a tal: element accepts TAL attributes only, so the marker above is a text line
rather than an attribute; prose in a template must never contain angle brackets,
because a tag name mentioned in passing is parsed as real markup and silently
reshapes the document; and the root element below must be closed -- Chameleon
accepts an unclosed one without complaint, but the enclosing define scope
collapses and every name in it reads as undefined at render time.
</tal:root>
